# Project-specific Pysa models for ReversecLabs/damn-vulnerable-llm-agent (DVLA).
#
# Loaded together with the taint.config in THIS directory (set
# `taint_models_path: ["projects/dvla"]`), so the bundled example models are not
# pulled in and verification stays clean against DVLA's source.
#
# WHAT DVLA LOOKS LIKE (from main.py / tools.py / transaction_db.py):
#   * LLM        : langchain_litellm.ChatLiteLLM  (a BaseChatModel)
#   * agent      : ConversationalChatAgent + AgentExecutor  (ReAct)
#   * tools      : registered via Tool(func=get_current_user / get_transactions)
#                  — NOT @tool-decorated, so the ModelQuery in frameworks.pysa
#                  does not apply; we model the functions explicitly below.
#   * dangerous  : TransactionDb.get_user_transactions builds RAW SQL by f-string
#                  (`SELECT * FROM Transactions WHERE userId = '{userId}'`) and
#                  runs it -> SQL sink. The userId is chosen by the agent.
#
# KEY POINT about real ReAct agents: the wiring "LLM output -> tool argument" is
# performed INSIDE LangChain's AgentExecutor (it parses the model output into an
# AgentAction and calls tool.run(tool_input)). None of that is in user code, so a
# naive scan sees no `.invoke` and no `@tool`. To attribute the tool argument to
# the LLM we therefore model the framework boundary (see LEVEL 2).

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# SQL sinks — DVLA's own DB-layer methods (user-defined methods verify cleanly;
# we avoid hand-writing the stdlib sqlite3.Cursor.execute signature).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def transaction_db.TransactionDb.get_user_transactions(self, userId: TaintSink[SQL]): ...
def transaction_db.TransactionDb.get_user(self, user_id: TaintSink[SQL]): ...

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Tool input as the taint source.
#
# LEVEL 1 (explicit SQL injection, CWE-89) — DEFAULT.
#   Demonstrates the pipeline end-to-end on real code and the general
#   inter-procedural flow (tools.get_transactions -> TransactionDb.get_user_-
#   transactions across modules). The tool argument is attacker/LLM controllable.
#   The resulting finding does NOT carry `llm_node`, so the post-processor
#   classifies it as an explicit (verbatim) flow.
def tools.get_transactions(userId: TaintSource[ToolOutput, Via[cap_string]]): ...

# LEVEL 2 (implicit / cross-tool, CWE-1426) — comment LEVEL 1 above and use this.
#   In a ReAct AgentExecutor the tool ARGUMENT is produced by the model and the
#   dispatch is internal to LangChain. We model that boundary directly by tagging
#   the tool input as LLM-routed (`Via[llm_node]`). A flow from here to the SQL
#   sink is then reported as an IMPLICIT (control-dependency) flow. This is a
#   deliberate modeling choice for framework-mediated agents; document it.
# def tools.get_transactions(userId: TaintSource[ToolOutput, Via[llm_node]]): ...

# tools.get_current_user returns user data; it calls get_user(1) with a constant,
# so it introduces no attacker taint (kept here for completeness / negative case).
# def tools.get_current_user(input) -> TaintSource[ToolOutput, Via[cap_string]]: ...

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# LEVEL 2 (advanced) — fully faithful threading through the LLM call.
# If you want the flow to pass through the actual model call instead of asserting
# llm_node at the tool boundary, model ChatLiteLLM as the LLM node:
# def langchain_litellm.ChatLiteLLM.invoke(self, input: TaintInTaintOut[Via[llm_node]]): ...
# NOTE: connecting the model's output to the tool function's argument still
# requires modeling LangChain's AgentExecutor -> Tool.run -> func dispatch (the
# §4.2 "bridge"), which is higher-order and version-sensitive. Validate with
# `pyre validate-models` and iterate.
